  "resolved_items": [
    {
      "item": "judge classifications differ",
      "resolution": "Resolve as faithful-equivalent for the selected cell-average definition in its inherited one-dimensional context. The disagreement arises from an essential measure identification unavailable in the blind dossier but supplied explicitly in the native environment supplement. This conclusion follows from that evidence, not agreement counts.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5, Section 1.2 defines the average as the cell integral divided by cell volume. D003 gives reciprocal interval length times the integral. The supplement's fully explicit Real.volume_Ioc identifies the exact measure used in D003 and its interval normalization."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge requested adjudication",
      "resolution": "The requested investigation establishes both the identity of the integration measure and endpoint nullity. The hypothetical rescaled or atomic measure responsible for the request is excluded by the supplied native declarations.",
      "primary_evidence": "Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id explicitly identifies @MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume Real Real.measureSpace with StieltjesFunction.id.measure. StieltjesFunction.id has underlying function id. Real.volume_Icc gives volume (Icc a a) = ENNReal.ofReal (a - a) = 0."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge classification is undetermined",
      "resolution": "The undetermined judgment was justified by its restricted evidence, but its stated dependency on identifying the measure is now discharged. No independent mismatch remains in the selected definition, so the final classification is faithful-equivalent.",
      "primary_evidence": "The round-trip judgment identifies the measure as its decisive unresolved issue. Independently, D001–D003 and D060 yield the spatial integral quotient once combined with the supplement's explicit measure identity and interval-volume declarations."
    },
    {
      "item": "blind translator left dependency_coverage item D025 unclear",
      "resolution": "D025 is resolved. Its inferInstance selects measureSpaceOfInnerProductSpace on the usual real normed inner-product space and Borel measurable space. Its volume is ordinary spatial Lebesgue measure with unit interval length normalization. It is neither a free measure parameter nor an unidentified multiple of length measure.",
      "primary_evidence": "The supplement expands Real.measureSpace to @inferInstance with @measureSpaceOfInnerProductSpace Real and expands that construction to the orthonormal-basis Haar measure. The Borel output gives Real.measurableSpace = borel ℝ. Fully explicit Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id and Real.volume_Ioc use @MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume Real Real.measureSpace, the same expression appearing in the target."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C04 unclear",
      "resolution": "C04 passes. Integrability is with respect to the source's spatial measure. Strict endpoint ordering and interval integrability make the conventional finite quotient meaningful; they are analytic precisifications rather than source-stated assumptions or grounds for stronger classification. No smoothness, global integrability, or linearity premise is introduced.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 use spatial dx integration and explicitly accommodate discontinuities. D007 requires IntegrableOn on Ioc left right and Ioc right left. Under hcell the latter set is empty, and the supplement identifies the former measure as spatial length measure."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C05 unclear",
      "resolution": "C05 passes for the selected defining clause. Both the numerator and normalization concern the same spatial cell. The nearby flux-update discussion explains use of the average and is not an additional conclusion of this selected definition.",
      "primary_evidence": "The source locator selects the defining clause in Section 1.2 on printed page 5. D001 requires equality to D003; D002 supplies D003 without changing state or endpoints. Real.volume_real_Ioc_of_le identifies that cell's real volume with right - left."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C06 unclear",
      "resolution": "C06 passes. The local predicate packages endpoint order, integrability, and equality; the abbreviation supplies the normalized integral; intervalIntegral is the difference of the two restricted integrals. The measure supplement resolves the previously opaque projection. The scalar-action and subtraction instance chains preserve ordinary real-vector operations.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001–D003 give the complete local bodies; D007 and D060 expose integrability and integration regions; D009 projects the measure resolved in D025. D018 uses Pi.module, and D047–D059 expose the operation and instance projections for reciprocal-length scalar multiplication and subtraction."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C10 unclear",
      "resolution": "C10 passes within the selected immediate interval context. There is no arbitrary-measure generalization. Fin m → Real preserves all finite real components, and an arbitrary state can represent any fixed-time slice. The m = 0 case is a harmless definitional extension. This target does not establish a multidimensional averaging theorem and receives no stronger classification.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ; equation (1.10) on printed page 4 integrates between real spatial endpoints. The target quantifies over m, state, and endpoints and fixes Real.measureSpace.volume. D004–D005 provide finite coordinate indexing, while D016–D019 supply the finite product structures."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item C11 unclear",
      "resolution": "C11 passes. Positive length excludes zero-volume cells and reversed endpoint representations. The half-open convention changes neither integrability nor the integral because endpoints have zero spatial measure. The theorem's hypotheses avoid nonintegrable-input conventions; it makes no cell-average assertion for excluded inputs.",
      "primary_evidence": "The target assumes left < right and IntervalIntegrable. D060 integrates over Ioc left right minus Ioc right left. The supplement gives Real.volume_Icc, which makes singleton volume zero, and Real.volume_Ioc, which gives positive finite cell volume when left < right."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left semantic_checklist item N06 unclear",
      "resolution": "N06 passes. The identified spatial measure, interval integrability, positive finite width, and complete finite real-vector codomain support the ordinary normalized integral. The target adds no PDE existence or uniqueness claim and requires no differentiability.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5 defines a finite-volume average in a discussion including discontinuous solutions. The explicit target fixes Fin m → Real with its finite product structures and assumes interval integrability. The supplement identifies interval volume as right - left, and D003 divides by this positive scalar."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication translation_implies_source unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve to yes after identifying the translation's fixed μ with the exact spatial measure established by the supplement. Substituting state x = q(x,t) at any fixed time yields the source's integral divided by the same cell's volume, in every component.",
      "primary_evidence": "Equation (1.10) on printed page 4 integrates q(x,t) against dx. Printed page 5 supplies the normalization. D003 and D060 supply the corresponding expression, while the fully explicit Real.volume_Ioc and Real.volume_eq_stieltjes_id identify its measure and normalization."
    },
    {
      "item": "round-trip judge left implication source_implies_translation unclear",
      "resolution": "Resolve to yes for the selected definition with the stated analytic precisification. For any target inputs satisfying its premises, the source quotient is exactly the vector named by D002. D001 requires that equality and the two already supplied premises. No independent computed value, extra estimate, or additional witness must be obtained from the source.",
      "primary_evidence": "Printed page 5 defines the exact average by the integral quotient. D002 abbreviates D003, and D001 conjoins equality to D003 with hcell and interval integrability. The supplement establishes that the integral and divisor have the source's spatial meanings."
    },
    {
      "item": "Nonvacuity, definitional reflexivity, and remaining dependency semantics",
      "resolution": "Nonvacuity passes: m = 1, left = 0, right = 1, and state x i = x give admissible nonconstant data with average 1/2. Nonzero constant fields and integrable jump fields also remain admissible. The reflexive equality is appropriate because the selected source claim is itself a definition, and its defining body has been independently checked. The finite-product norm's unexpanded formula creates no material uncertainty: the target asserts no norm estimate, and equivalent finite-dimensional norms preserve integrability and the vector integral. The remaining supplied dependencies introduce only standard finite indexing, real algebra, logical conjunction/equality, and compatible structure projections.",
      "primary_evidence": "D001–D003 expose the definitional equality; D004–D005 and D010 allow positive dimensions; D016–D019 give finite real product structures; D024 includes real completeness; D032–D033 retain exact conjunction and equality. The native interval-volume declarations ensure a nonzero finite measure on (0,1]. Printed pages 4–5 supply the integral and discontinuity context."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "lean_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "For the selected definition in its immediate one-dimensional context, fix time t and an admissible interval cell with left < right, and instantiate state x = q(x,t). D001–D003 give the exact average as (right - left)⁻¹ acting on D060's interval integral. The reverse interval is empty. The native supplement identifies the exact measure argument as spatial Lebesgue measure and the same cell's real volume as right - left. Endpoint choices are immaterial because singletons have zero measure. Thus the target and its defining dependencies represent the source quotient componentwise. This does not assert the surrounding numerical update process or a multidimensional theorem."
    },
    "source_implies_lean": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "On the target's positive-length, interval-integrable finite real-vector inputs, the source definition assigns precisely the spatial integral divided by interval volume. The supplied native declarations identify that volume with right - left, so D002 through D003 names the assigned vector. D001 then asks for the defining equality together with the already assumed order and integrability. Fixed-time instantiation and finite coordinate functions preserve the intended objects. The empty-vector case follows definitionally and contributes no substantive additional strength."
    }
  },
  "classification": "faithful-equivalent",
  "accepted": true,
  "remaining_uncertainties": [],
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "resolved-integration-measure",
      "evidence": "The blind dossier exposed D025 only as inferInstance. The native supplement explicitly identifies its selected instance, Borel structure, identity-Stieltjes measure, and interval-volume formula for the exact measure argument used by the target.",
      "impact": "The round-trip measure uncertainty is discharged by declaration evidence; provenance hashes alone were not used to infer semantic correctness."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "analytic-precisification-and-scope",
      "evidence": "The source defining clause supplies no formal admissibility list. The target requires positive interval length and interval integrability and represents the field at a fixed time in one spatial dimension.",
      "impact": "Acceptance concerns the conventional finite interval-average definition. These conditions are mathematical precisifications, not a claim that the source explicitly lists them or that the target proves a stronger theorem."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "definitional-specification",
      "evidence": "D002 abbreviates D003, and D001 repeats admissibility together with equality to D003.",
      "impact": "The specification is definitionally reflexive, but the checked defining expression matches the selected source definition and has nontrivial admissible instances."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Independent inspection of the attached primary-source pages and inline declaration evidence establishes the same spatial normalized integral on both sides. The decisive supplement identifies the exact Real.measureSpace.volume used by the target, including its normalization and endpoint nullity, resolving every measure-dependent trigger. Both semantic implication directions hold for the selected one-dimensional definition with ordinary analytic admissibility made explicit. No reduced applicability is mislabeled as stronger, and neither empty vectors nor definitional reflexivity conceal a mismatch. I used no tools, accessed no external files or target proof, and did not independently recompute the supplied provenance hashes."
}
